                </svg>
            </button>
        </div>

       <div class="ch">
<h1>RC Charge / Discharge with an SPST Switch</h1>

<div class="ch-card">
<h2>Circuit Components</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Source (V1):</strong> DC voltage source, 10 V</li>
<li><strong>Switch (SW1):</strong> SPST manual switch between V1 and R1</li>
<li><strong>Resistor (R1):</strong> 1 kΩ (charging resistor)</li>
<li><strong>Capacitor (C1):</strong> 100 µF, initially discharged</li>
<li><strong>Resistor (R2):</strong> 5 kΩ, always connected across C1 (discharge path)</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Why R2 Is There</h2>
<p>An SPST switch only connects or disconnects the source. When it is open, C1 would keep its charge forever, so R2 gives the capacitor a path to discharge. The price is that R2 also loads the circuit while the switch is closed, so C1 does not reach the full 10 V.</p>

<h2>Charging: Thevenin Equivalent Seen by C1</h2>
<div class="ch-formula">
